# Files allowed to contain `[insert … here]` placeholders.
#
# WHY this exists: the placeholder gate (pre-commit + CI) blocks unreplaced
# `[insert … here]` markers so a team never ships them into real code/config.
# But some files legitimately contain those markers forever:
#   - meta files that DOCUMENT the placeholder convention (e.g. AGENTS.md § 10);
#   - template files filled in per-project (manifest, integration map, task template).
#
# One shell glob per line (matched against the repo-relative path). Lines starting
# with # are comments. Keep this list TIGHT — every entry is a file the gate no
# longer protects. When you adopt the starter and replace placeholders, you can
# remove entries you no longer need (the templates you've filled in).

# Convention documentation (these describe the placeholders, so they keep them):
AGENTS.md
ADOPTING.md
CLAUDE.md
.githooks/README.md

# The enforcement machinery itself references the literal pattern it scans for:
.githooks/pre-commit
scripts/ci-checks.sh
scripts/test/test-ci-checks.sh
.github/workflows/checks.yml
.github/workflows-disabled/checks.yml
.placeholder-allow

# Per-project templates (filled in during adoption; may carry placeholders meanwhile):
tasks/_TEMPLATE.md

# Specialist role briefs reference stack placeholders teams replace on adoption:
.claude/agents/*.md

# Seeded bootstrap/adoption tasks reference placeholders by name:
tasks/T-001*.md
tasks/T-002.md
